//? Title: Random-restart hill climbing: actual proposals, accepted moves and grid references
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A multimodal objective can trap a local coordinate search at a poor peak. Random
restarts explore additional starting basins while retaining the best actually
visited objective value. The native producer now records its real starts, signed
proposals, bound skips and accepted states. The original sine-mixture objective,
sixty starts and seed1 remain, with a dense grid maximum labelled as a finite
reference rather than an exact global certificate.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

From the current point, the producer tries each coordinate at +step then-step,
immediately accepting a strictly larger finite objective. A complete sweep with
no improvement halves the step. It stops a climb when step is at most the
configured minimum, then keeps the best restart result.
$$ f(x_{accepted}^{k+1})>f(x_{accepted}^{k}),\qquad f_{best}^{r+1}\ge f_{best}^{r}. $$
\alttext(Accepted moves strictly improve the objective, and extending the same restart prefix cannot lower the best visited value.)
These are native decision invariants, not a guarantee of a global maximum. An
actual bounded box can exclude a signed proposal; that record has no objective
evaluation. A fixed seed determines the start sequence, and callbacks can have
their own cost or side effects. Evaluation/history budgets bound selected
execution; positive step thresholds and no-improvement halvings are explicit. A
grid search samples only its listed coordinates and can miss a peak between
them.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/random_restart_hill_climbing_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    a::HillClimbOptions o;
    o.restarts = 1;
    auto t = a::hill_climb_trace(a::hill_wave_objective, {0}, {2}, o);
    const auto &initial = t.steps[0];
    const auto &proposal = t.steps[1];
    std::print("\n| selected actual record (first12 only) | initial | evaluated | accepted | before | " "attempted point | after | actual retained value |\n| ---: | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | " "--- | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < std::min<std::size_t>(12, t.steps.size()); ++i) {
        const auto &s = t.steps[i];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | `{}` | `{}` | `{}` | {} |\n", i,
                   s.initial, s.evaluated, s.accepted, s.before, s.candidate,
                   s.after, s.value_after);
    }

    g::Diagram d;
    d.node("n0", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual random initial point\n{}", initial.after));
    d.node("n1", {10, 0},
           std::format("Actual first signed proposal accepted\n{}",
                       proposal.accepted));
    d.node("n2", {20, 0},
           std::format("Actual best visited point / value\n{} / {}", t.best.x,
                       t.best.value));
    d.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    d.save_svg("build/random_restart_hill_climbing_tiny.svg");
}
```
