> API and interpretation

The original dense scan was called a global optimum, although it evaluates only
a finite grid. It is now labelled accordingly, and the native curve/scan helpers
preserve its repeated-addition sampling order. Search can exceed a grid maximum
between grid coordinates without contradicting either result. The first tiny
table is explicitly a prefix of actual records, not reruns with different
budgets. The legacy callable template still supports by-value move-only
callables through a borrowed synchronous adapter. Callback effects and
exceptions propagate; no rollback or global-optimality guarantee is supplied.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

